NotesString/You can open this notes window for any project by selecting View->Notes...

The data in this project was acquired from a CNC mill as it was receiving 
instructions from a PC over its RS232 interface.

Because the mill was completing the instructions in real time (cutting paths for
instance), the data is sparse with much dead-time between transitions. This is
the ideal scenario for sample compression. 

You can see that more than 6 seconds of data has been acquired at a sample
rate of 100MHz, providing a sample resolution of 10ns. The result was over 
600 million samples of data compressed into the LogicPort's sample buffer. 
This 300,000:1 ratio is an extreme example of what sample compression can do!

"Zoom In" to see 10ns resolution, "Zoom All" to see the total sampled data range.

Notice that you can measure the actual baud rate of the data transmitted 
from the PC (between cursors A/B) and the data echoed back from the CNC 
mill (between cursors C/D):

   The PC's actual baud rate was 2/208.34us or 9599.7 baud

   The CNC mill's actual baud rate was 2/208.42us or 9596.0 baud

We have measured two consecutive bits because the marks and spaces 
generated by this particular RS232 interface are not symmetrical.

This example also demonstrates the Asynchronous Serial (RS232) Interpreter. 
As a result you can see the actual data transmitted to the CNC mill, translated
into ASCII characters. Be sure to take a look at the State List tab for this
example. The state list can be particularly helpful when using interpreters.

Note that a conventional logic analyzer, even with memory 2 megasamples 
deep, could not capture this much data at this resolution. A conventional logic 
analyzer continues to sample and fill its memory even when no transitions are 
occurring in the data. The majority of its memory would be filled with copies of 
identical data.

Please note that all of the example files are "read only" so that they remain 
intact for future reference. You can change settings in these files for learning 
purposes, but the changes cannot be saved. You can however use the 
"Save As" option to save an editable copy of any example file.
/
